Summary: Run mvn clean install

Reasoning:
Okay, so I've just reviewed the latest OSV scan report, `osv-scan-report-5.json`, and I'm seeing a couple of lingering vulnerabilities that I really need to address.

First, there's the `org.apache.logging.log4j:log4j-api` dependency, specifically at version `2.25.4`. The report flags this with `GHSA-qv9r-c865-cp47`, which maps to CVE-2026-49844. The 
good news is that the fix is available, with corrected versions being `2.25.5` or `2.26.1`.

Second, I'm seeing an issue with `org.springframework:spring-webmvc`, identified by `GHSA-pc63-qcmh-9cmg`, or CVE-2026-47884. This vulnerability affects versions from `7.0.0` all the way 
up to `7.0.8`. The fix here is version `7.0.9`.

Now, here's where I've already taken action. I've proactively managed these dependencies. For `log4j-api`, I've explicitly set the version to `2.25.5` within my `dependencyManagement` 
section. Similarly, for `spring-webmvc`, I've also pinned it to the fixed version, `7.0.9`, in the same `dependencyManagement` area. I also recall doing the same for `spring-webflux`, 
ensuring it's on version `7.0.9` as well, which I had done prior to this review.

The next crucial step is to confirm that these changes have indeed resolved the issues and haven't introduced any regressions. To do that, I'm going to run a `mvn clean install`. This 
will ensure that the build process completes successfully with these updated dependency versions in place. I expect it to pass, but I need to verify.

$ mvn clean install

